Understanding the Master Theorem
================================

(September 23rd, 2026)

When I first saw this in class last year, it confused me a lot because of the arbitrary cases. But I hope to convey an intuitive explanation.

The Master Theorem is a trick to quickly calculate the time complexity for recursive functions. The (simplified) version we learn goes like this:

Suppose T(n), the time complexity  of an algorithm, is of the form T(n) = aT(n/b) + O(n^d). Then T(n) =
  n^log(a)       if n^log(a) > n^d
  n^d            if n^log(a) < n^d
  n^d * log(n)   if n^log(a) = n^d

But why is this the case?

First of all, notice that the cases are actually not as scary as they first appear. They are just the bigger of  n^log(a)  and  n^d  if the two numbers are distinct, and n^d * log(n) if the answers given by the other two cases are the same.

Let's consider the simpler relation T(n) = aT(n/b), and find the base case when n = 1. So we want to see how many times we can divide n by b until we reach 1. This is just log_b(n). Now each of those log_b(n) times we multiply by "a", giving us a time complexity of O(a^log_b(n)). The reader can verify that a^log_b(n) = n^log_b(a).

Putting this all together with T(n) = aT(n/b) + O(n^d), the recursive part ("aT(n/b)") has time complexity O(n^log(a)), and the O(n^d) part has time complexity, well, O(n^d). So we compare these two numbers to figure out the time complexity, take the bigger one, and we're done!


... hold your horses. The theorem gives another case when they're equal. Why is this?

You may have noticed that what I said above isn't *entirely* accurate. The O(n^d) part isn't just called a constant number of times, it's actually called once per iteration of the algorithm, for a total of log_b(n) times. Also, each time the algorithm is recursed into, the argument gets smaller, and we multiply by "a" on the outside. So it really goes:

O(n^d)  +  a * O((n/b)^d)  +  a^2 * O((n/b^2)^d)  +  ...

where the first corresponds with T(n), the second with a * T(n/b), the third with a^2 * T(n/b^2), etc. So this sum is

log_b(n) - 1
    \--       i   d   -id
     >       a * n * b
    /--
   i = 0

       log_b(n) - 1      i
    d      \--      ( a )
 = n   *    >       (---)
           /--      (b^d)
          i = 0

Recalling the formula

   k            (k+1)
  \--   i      x      - 1
   >   x   =   -----------
  /--             x - 1
 i = 0

we get that the sum is equal to

             log_b(n)
 d     (a/b^d)        - 1
n  *   ------------------
            a/b^d - 1

since the denominator doesn't rely on n and we ignore constant factors when analyzing time complexity, we can completely disregard it.

     (  log_b(n)      )
 d   ( a              )
n  * ( ---------- - 1 )
     (  dlog_b(n)     )
     ( b              )

          log_b(n)     d
    d    a          - n
=  n  *  --------------
                d
               n

   log_b(n)    d
= a         - n

So we find that the O(n^d) part actually leads to a total time complexity of max { a^log_b(n), n^d }. Which, since we already have the aT(n/d) part giving a time complexity of a^log_b(n), is the same as our naive assumption.

BUT WAIT!! The summation formula we used only works for x != 1. Otherwise, we'd be dividing by zero.
In the case that x = 1, we get that the sum is simply equal to k (in this case, log_b(n)). So if the whole sum is equal to log_b(n), then we get that the time complexity is n^d * log_b(n) when a/b^d = 1; in other words, when a = b^d, or when log_b(a) = d. Thus giving us the final piece of the formula.

So here's how I like to think about it:

T(n) = aT(n/b) + O(n^d)

the time complexity is:

  max { a^log(n), n^d }    if those two values are distinct (recall a^log_b(n) = n^log_b(a))
  n^d * log(n)             if the previous values were the same
